WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:11.180
Between 2007 and 2018, outstanding
student debt is nearly tripled to $1.5

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.240
trillion, and many worry it's the next
financial bubble like the housing crisis

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.830
of the 2000s. Observers are right to
worry about a student debt bubble, but

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.070
it's not like the housing crisis.
Specifically, the federal government

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.480
already backs 90% of student debt. So bad
loans aren't about to bring down banks.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.980
And while taxpayers bear the cost of bad
loans, that's not where the big risks are,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.670
either. Most delinquent and defaulted
loans are small, and the government

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:40.230
usually recovers most of the money.
Instead, the real risk of a financial

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.280
bubble come from income-based repayment,
a program where borrowers pay a small

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.879
share of their earnings, no matter their
debt loads, and then have remaining

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:53.789
balances forgiven after 10 or 20 years.
Costs for this program have skyrocketed, with

00:00:53.789 --> 00:00:57.719
debt for graduate and professional degrees
dominating the program, because those

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:01.980
students can borrow virtually unlimited
federal loans. And unlimited loans and

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.620
loan forgiveness for graduate school,
is the real student debt bubble.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:11.220
Do you think there's a student debt bubble?
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.000
know what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.460
sure to LIKE and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

